---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Prompt Engineering Basics - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="fundamentals" subChapterId="prompt-engineering">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				Prompt engineering is just a fancy term for writing out what you want. Much of this guide will ultimately come down to prompt engineering, but in this page we'd like to cover a few of the basics.
			</p>

			<p>
				As tasks get more involved you'll also hear this called <strong class="text-stone-800">context engineering</strong>: less about the exact words in one message, more about assembling the right files, examples, and background so the model has what it needs. We go deeper on that in the <a href="/development/prompting-for-development">development chapter</a>. The basics below apply either way.
			</p>

			<h3>
				The Why Matters More Than the What
			</h3>

			<p>
				The single biggest improvement you can make to your prompts is explaining <strong
					class="text-stone-800">why</strong
				> you need something, not just what you need. When the model understands the purpose behind your
				request, it can ask better questions back, as well as "know" to test the solution in different ways.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-red-400 mb-1 line-through decoration-red-300">
						"Write a function to parse dates"
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						The model will pick a random format, a random library, and make assumptions
						about edge cases. You'll probably need to go back and forth several times to get it right.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-green-700 mb-1">
						"We're parsing user-submitted dates from a form that accepts free-text input. Users
						enter dates in various formats (MM/DD/YYYY, January 5 2024, etc). We need a
						TypeScript function that normalizes these to ISO 8601. It should return null for
						unparseable input rather than throwing. If there is a good library to help with this please suggest it before implementing it yourself."
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						Clear purpose, specific constraints, defined behavior for edge cases. The model has
						everything it needs to produce something usable on the first try.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<h3>
				Set Goals, Not Just Instructions
			</h3>

			<p>
				Models are literal and obedient. They tend to do exactly what you say, even when what you said isn't quite right. That's fine when you know the precise change you want, but it works against you when you don't. If you dictate the mechanics, the model won't second-guess them, even if a better solution was one step away.
			</p>

			<p>
				Say a form submission is failing. If you tell the agent "add a null check in <code>submitForm.ts</code>", that's exactly what you'll get: a null check in that one file. But if the real cause is that the API returns an error the frontend never handles, you've just papered over the symptom. State the goal instead, "form submissions are silently failing when the server rejects them, make them fail gracefully with a visible error", and the agent is free to investigate, trace the actual cause, and touch whatever files it needs to fix it properly.
			</p>

			<p>
				The rule of thumb: instruct when you're certain of the <em>how</em>, set a goal when you care about the <em>outcome</em> and want the agent to work out the how. Goals still need guardrails (you review what it changed), but they let the model bring its judgment to bear instead of just following orders off a cliff.
			</p>

			<h3>
				Link to Real Examples
			</h3>

			<p>
				One of the most effective prompting techniques is providing <strong class="text-stone-800"
					>golden masters</strong
				>: examples of exactly the kind of output you want. If you need the AI to write a test, show
				it an existing test that follows your team's conventions. If you need a requirements document,
				link to one that's already been approved.
			</p>

			<p>
				The model is excellent at pattern matching. Give it a concrete example and say "follow this
				pattern" and you'll get dramatically more consistent results than describing the pattern in
				words. This is especially true for formatting, naming conventions, and code style: things
				that are tedious to describe but obvious from an example.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Golden masters">
				<p>
					Keep a collection of "best examples" for your common tasks: a well-written ticket, a
					clean test file, a properly structured requirements doc. When you need the AI to produce
					something similar, include the example in your prompt. This is one of the highest-leverage
					habits you can build.
				</p>
				<p>
					You can also list these golden masters in skills, rules or the agents file where appropriate.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>
				Keep Tasks Bite-Sized
			</h3>

			<p>
				AI models handle focused, well-scoped tasks far better than broad, ambiguous ones. "Refactor
				our entire authentication system" will produce a lot of code all at once that may or may not work, and will require a lot of review and testing. "Add input validation to the login
				form's email field..." will produce something useful.
			</p>

			<p>
				This isn't a limitation to work around. It's actually a better way to work. Breaking large
				tasks into small, clear steps gives you control over the process and lets you verify each
				piece before moving on. You catch problems early instead of discovering them buried in a
				massive generated changeset.
			</p>

			<h3>
				Tell It to Ask Questions First
			</h3>

			<p>
				One of the simplest ways to reduce bad assumptions is to tell the AI to ask you
				clarifying questions before it starts working. A phrase like "before you begin, ask me
				any questions you need answered" can move these items up front even before the plan is written, and save you entire rounds of back-and-forth.
			</p>

			<p>
				The model will fill in the blanks with its best guesses because it wants to be helpful. Sometimes those
				guesses are right, but when they're wrong you end up with output that looks polished
				but misses what you intended. By inviting questions up front, you give the model a chance to
				surface the ambiguities you didn't realize you had.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-green-700 mb-1">
						"I need to add error handling to our API endpoints. Before you start, ask me any
						questions about our current setup, conventions, or requirements."
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						The model might ask: which endpoints? Do you have a standard error response
						format? Should errors be logged? Do you use a global error handler? Each answer
						steers it closer to what you actually need.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<p>
				For a packaged version of this habit, Matt Pocock's <a href="https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/main/skills/productivity/grill-me" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-orange-600 underline hover:text-orange-700">grill-me skill</a> is a great example: it interviews you relentlessly about a plan or design, walking down every branch of the decision tree and offering a recommended answer for each question, until you've resolved the ambiguities before any work starts.
			</p>

			<p>
				This can also help you identify gaps in your skills or docs, so note those down for later if you think something might be worth documenting.
			</p>

			<h3>
				Ask for a Plan First
			</h3>

			<p>
				For anything non-trivial, have the model lay out its plan before it touches any code. "Draft a plan for how you'd approach this, then wait for my go-ahead" lets you catch a wrong direction while it's still a paragraph you can edit, rather than a changeset you have to unwind. It's the cheapest place to correct course. Most tools have this built in now, and we cover it properly in <a href="/development/agent-plan-ask">Agent, Plan & Ask Modes</a>.
			</p>

			<h3>
				Reference What You Want Done
			</h3>

			<p>
				The model works best when it has very clear instructions. Where possible, you should reference the actual code, documents, or data it's working on. This will allow it to skip any steps about finding code or documents and will reduce the overall context window required to complete the task, which should in turn improve the quality of the output.
			</p>

			<p>
				Tools like Cursor and Claude Code can automatically read your
				project files, see your Git history, and understand your codebase structure. But they still
				benefit from you pointing them in the right direction: "look at the auth middleware in
				src/middleware/auth.ts" is better than "look at the auth code."
			</p>

			<h3>
				Quick Reference
			</h3>

			<div class="space-y-3 my-8">
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div
						class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"
					>
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">State the why</strong>: purpose and context before the
						specific request.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div
						class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"
					>
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Set goals, not just orders</strong>: describe the outcome when you want the agent to find the right fix, not just the one you guessed.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div
						class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"
					>
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Show, don't describe</strong>: link to real files and
						examples rather than describing them.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div
						class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"
					>
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">One task at a time</strong>: focused requests get
						focused results.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div
						class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"
					>
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Specify the format</strong>: tell it how you want the
						output structured.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div
						class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"
					>
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Invite questions first</strong>: let the model ask
						what it needs before it starts working.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div
						class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"
					>
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Ask for a plan</strong>: on non-trivial work, review the approach before any code gets written.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div
						class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"
					>
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Define edge cases</strong>: tell it what should happen
						with invalid input, errors, or unusual scenarios.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div
						class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"
					>
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Use golden masters</strong>: provide examples of what
						"good" looks like for your context.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<p>
				Prompt engineering can largely be thought of as good and deep requirements writing. Remember the <a href="/fundamentals/thinking-of-the-ai">mental model</a>: you're briefing someone new every single time. The better your brief, the better the output.
			</p>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
